Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 14:35:52 -0500
From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

Also, there are large societies that are completely vegetarian.
Hindus, for starters. Jains and some Budhhists are vegans.

While other factors are not close enough to compare lifespans, particularly
economic wealth and availability of medical care, it certainly is
possible to survive on a vegetarian diet. High protein consumption
correlates with larger mass, but there is some evidence that larger
mass is NOT correlated with longer or healthier life.
   
I've been a vegetarian and a meat-eater, and it doesn't seem to me
that there is that big of a difference between the healthy vegetarian
diet and the healthy meat-eating diet. The important part seems to be
to switch the emphasis in the diet from large hunks of meat to large
quantities of whole grains and vegetables; whether you get your fat
from milk or meat or safflower oil hasn't been shown to be as
important as cutting down the fat, and it is impossible to have a
decent-sized piece of meat two or three times a day and still get
dietary fat down.
   The other issue in our country is the quality of the meat. I
wouldn't worry so much if our meat came from small farms, but it comes
from huge factories where the animals are kept in confinement, pumped
with huge doses of hormones and antibiotics, fed grains that are
treadet with pesticides not allowed on crops intended for humans, sprayed with
industrial-strength pesticides, stacked vertically so that they are
also covered in dung, and shipped without food or water for their last
36-48 hours. On its way to our food, the meat may go through several
stages of shipping, partial thawing, storage, processing, re-storage,
handling, etc.
  Even if you discount any "humanitarian" concerns, we are dealing
with a meat supply that is heavily contaminated, a chicken supply
where between 50 and 95% of all samples are contaminated with
salmonella, a pork supply that is not safe to eat without cooking at
high temperatures, and a massive concentration of pesticides and
hormones. (and a government which is not capable of or willing to
maintain any sort of quality control, but has an advertising budget)
   Not exactly great stuff. You still get the pesticides by eating
commercailly raised grains, but in much lower concentrations and
without the antibiotics and bacteria.




