Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 11:38:48 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

Hal Finney writes:
>Tim makes the good point that there are many areas where a lack of
>intellectual property protection has not stopped innovation, that
>doesn't really prove anything.  Perhaps there could be even more
>innovation if intellectual property were protected in these fields.

So once we're willing to set aside moral axioms, the question comes
down to whether we are better off on average, in the ordinary economic
sense, with or without patents (etc.).  Does the benefits of allowing
this form of property outweight the costs of defining and policing
these rights?  If yes, I'll support patents, if no I won't, here or in
a PPL.

This is largely an economics question.  The answer might be revealed
in the market, if we had relatively isolated comparable competing
continents with and without patents.  Competing PPLs might reveal the
answer, but then again they might not due to strong interaction
effects.  Absent such revelation, we are asked to form an economic
judgement on the basis of aneqdotes, fancy regressions, and high
econ math.  None of which is particularly compelling.  So I have to
come down with Tim against patents, due to my basic skepticism against
globally constraining local behavior without compelling reasons.

There is one market I might imagine deferring to.  Imagine creating
some reliable measure of useful inventive activity, and creating
betting markets on these measures, conditional on having or not having
various forms of patents, etc. 

Robin Hanson

