Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 15:15:46 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

> From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> Perry wrote:
> >After all, futures are a zero sum game, unlike, say, the equities market. 
> 
> I asked:
> > I think I disagree.  Can you offer a more precise definition of
> > "zero-sum" so that we could evaluate your claim?  Both futures and
> > equities offer a net return on one's investment, returns competitive
> > with each other when corrected for market-correlated risk.  
> 
> Perry responded:
> >Equities are shares in companies. If the company gets more valuable, it
> >is possible for an investor to get richer without anyone else getting
> >poorer. On the other hand, the futures market is a betting market in which
> >whenever I make money it is because someone else has lost it. 
> 
> This is not precise enough for me.  To ground your thinking, consider
> the following observations:
> 
> 1) As I said above, the net average return of futures investors is
> positive, not zero.  So they could *all* make money relative to where
> they started.

I can't believe that the net average return is positive -- it must be zero.

A futures contract is nothing but a bet on the future price of a commodity.
If I make money, it comes directly out of another players pocket -- exactly
as in insurance or in Las Vegas. I don't see that there is any way for the
average return to be positive. Some players have very positive return and
others very negative, but if one summed together all the trades across all
players the game would have to sum to zero.

> 2) If we ignore voting rights and dividends, owning a stock is
> equivalent to betting on a company's stock, if what we are betting is
> a index "market asset", an average of all stocks weighted by their
> current market value.   Your stock can only go up relative to the
> index if someone else's goes down.  Does this make stocks zero-sum?

I don't understand your point here. Could you clarify it?

Allow me to repeat my point again, though. If I am buying an index future
on the S&P 500 and the index goes up $10 per contract, I have made $10 --
but the person that sold that contract has lost it, because he must pay
me those $10. On the other hand, if I buy the S&P 500 as a basket, and
the index goes up $10, the person who sold me the basket has lost potential
income, but doesn't have to pay me anything -- the game is positive sum.

> 3) What if they offered futures on individual stocks?  Again ignoring
> dividends, the current futures price should just equal the current
> stock price.  There's no point in offering such futures, because
> there's no difference.  How then could one be zero-sum and the other
> not?

Actually such futures do exist, Robin -- in the form of synthetics generated
off of LEAPS, but they are none the less real. I recall that there were
also people talking about pure futures contracts on stocks, but that idea
never really went anywhere. Again, the futures contract (or options
contract) is a zero sum game but the actual equity is not. Remember, one
is a bet, and the other is a purchase of a tangible. There is a big difference
globally if what I have done is bought a house versus betting someone
on what the value of the house will be in six months. One is the aquisition
of a tangible asset -- no one need lose money on that. On the other hand,
someone always loses money in a bet, by definition.

Perry


